aligned}v(1) & =⟨{4}^{1-1},{9}^{1-1}⟩=⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=1$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(1)| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308316 13194139534760 true] [17592186308315 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534760 true] [17592186308315 1041 "field-1" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308315 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308316 13194139534760 true] [17592186308315 1110 #uuid "42c5db93-17cd-5de7-86d6-737e596f3050" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308316 1034 "\\sqrt{2}" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308316 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534760 true] [17592186308316 1111 #uuid "26edb362-29ef-5dc8-8294-2df22990b811" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308317 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308318 13194139534760 true] [17592186308317 1089 "q-352587" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308317 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534760 true] [17592186308317 1100 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534760 true] [17592186308317 1113 #uuid "67679c8a-32b4-5266-9ffd-bf77106d9a75" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308317 1145 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{2t+1},\\,\\frac{1}{t+1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2(1)+1},\\,\\frac{1}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=1$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(1)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308318 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308319 13194139534760 true] [17592186308318 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308320 13194139534760 true] [17592186308318 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534760 true] [17592186308318 1041 "field-1" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308318 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308319 13194139534760 true] [17592186308318 1110 #uuid "69ac440c-c275-5b9c-bb4e-c80f921cfff4" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308319 1034 "\\frac{5}{6}" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534760 true] [17592186308319 1111 #uuid "519e3928-5671-5cd0-9510-23e22caa7d2f" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308320 1034 "\\frac{7}{6}" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534760 true] [17592186308320 1111 #uuid "e2fb9704-94d7-5cda-8cda-568d7b42427c" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308321 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308322 13194139534760 true] [17592186308321 1089 "q-352722" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308321 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534760 true] [17592186308321 1100 "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨(t-6)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,({t}^{2}-36)^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534760 true] [17592186308321 1113 #uuid "300d663f-1d42-5d3b-99d8-47507ed9f5be" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308321 1145 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-6)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-1}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}-36)^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(t-6){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t(t-6)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(t-6)({t}^{2}-6t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2(t-6)({t}^{2}-6t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-6)({t}^{2}-6t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=6,\\text{ }3\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ 6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=6$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139534760 true] [17592186308322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308323 13194139534760 true] [17592186308322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308324 13194139534760 true] [17592186308322 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534760 true] [17592186308322 1041 "field-1" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308322 1104 #edb/ref 17592186308323 13194139534760 true] [17592186308322 1110 #uuid "9c62bea3-0021-5527-8d8b-aa124b36568c" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308323 1034 "6" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534760 true] [17592186308323 1111 #uuid "8e787af9-72a7-5ef5-9fb2-fc1ccef61359" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308324 1034 "0" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534760 true] [17592186308324 1111 #uuid "58ce2f09-6fa4-524b-b4d0-5d444e583556" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308325 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308326 13194139534760 true] [17592186308325 1089 "q-51321" 13194139534760 true] [17592186308325 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534760 true] [17592186308325 1100 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,